nd that  practicing the simon task  could improve a lacrosse player's  ability to target the ideal part of a  goal  this is something we could very easily  emulate in a training environment  by getting a subject to perform one of  two actions depending on the stimuli  and using various methods to interfere  with this message something else missing  from many video games is inhibitory  control  our ability to suppress inappropriate  reactions  the way a game might imitate this is by  presenting hostages and penalizing the  player for shooting them  this can be a very useful tool for  athletes and other high performers  in one study professional athletes  demonstrated superior performance  on a stop signal task this task requires  the individual to react quickly to a  stimulus  unless it is presented alongside a stop  signal a similar concept is the go  no-go task this is a skill that has very  obvious crossover for military personnel  in particular  or john wick in one study it was  demonstrated that actively training  response inhibition  could potentially reduce civilian  casualties the study used a go no-go  task a stop signal task a stroop task  and a visual search task to train  participants i believe this could be  even better if placed into a real  physical environment  developing quick reactions and decision  making and focus is one thing  maintaining that heightened cognitive  performance for long durations  is another the same challenge faces  drivers pilots  and surgeons working 10 hour shifts this  is psychomotor vigilance  but it's not just tiredness that can  cause lapses and focus on reactions  emotional stress can also have a huge  impact which is one reason we often  perform worse when under pressure  meditation has been shown to enhance  psychomotor vigilance  countless studies show how this can  result in greater emotional control  as the individual learns to ignore  distracting thoughts and feelings  but the military has another approach to  this 